{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And you shall make a breastplate of judgment, the work of a weaver.</b> He did not mention 'the work of a weaver' (מַעֲשֵׂה חוֹשֵׁב) for any of the garments except the ephod and the breastplate, because this indicates that this atonement is for a particular sin in which thought is accounted as deed — and this is found only in idolatrous worship, as was explained above in the matter of the ephod. And the breastplate, which comes to atone for the corruption of justice — for judgments are matters entrusted to the heart of the judge, since the judge has only what his eyes see, and it is in his power to call right left and left right, according to the circumstance, according to the person, according to the time, and according to the place. And if the judge says, 'Thus it appeared in my eyes,' who can contradict him — none but HaShem alone, who examines the hearts of the children of man? Therefore the breastplate was placed upon the heart of Aaron, for judgments are entrusted to the heart, and therefore its work was 'the work of a weaver' (מַעֲשֵׂה חוֹשֵׁב) to atone for the thought of the judge. And since it says 'the work of a weaver, like the work of the ephod,' it hung the matter of חוֹשֵׁב upon the ephod, to tell you that the corruption of justice is weighed equally against idolatrous worship, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 7:): 'Whoever appoints an unfit judge is as though he planted an asherah' and so forth — that is, idolatrous worship. Therefore it said that the breastplate of judgment, which atones for justice, shall be like the work of the ephod, which atones for idolatrous worship. And so our Sages of blessed memory interpreted the verse 'You shall not make with Me gods of silver' (Exodus 20:20) as referring to an unfit judge (Jerusalemite Bikkurim 3:3), for the essence of this sin too depends upon the thought of the judge, as it is written (Proverbs 12:5), 'The thoughts of the righteous are just' — for they are whole in justice even in their thought. 'It shall be square' — for every judge must be whole in four things enumerated in the verse 'And you shall seek out' and so forth (Exodus 18:21). 'It shall be doubled' — for his sin is also doubled, for he harms this one in his property and that one in his soul, for the one who holds his fellow's money unjustly — this is what is concluded in the midrash (Eikhah Rabbah 1:57): 'They sinned doubly and were smitten doubly' — for they sinned in the corruption of justice, as it is written (Isaiah 1:21), 'Righteousness lodged in her' and so forth, and they sinned in idolatrous worship, for this too is a doubled sin, as it is written (Jeremiah 2:13), 'They have forsaken Me, the fountain of living waters, to hew for themselves cisterns, broken cisterns.' And from all this we learn the likeness that the corruption of justice has to idolatrous worship; therefore the breastplate was like the work of the ephod. 'A span its length' — for the corruption of justice causes the destruction of the world, and its repair is as though one became a partner to the Holy One, blessed be He, in the work of Creation, of which it is written concerning them, 'And the heavens by a span He measured' (Isaiah 40:12)."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:24:01.997417509Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}